  <p>
    Most of the melanocortin obesity conditions break the <a href="/receptors/mc4r">appetite
    circuit</a> by removing a part of it — the <a href="/pomc-deficiency">precursor or its
    receptor</a>. <strong>Bardet–Biedl syndrome</strong> breaks it in a stranger, more
    revealing way: everything is present, but the receptors that read the appetite signal
    cannot get to the place they are supposed to work. It is a melanocortin disease by a
    back door, and following that back door turns out to explain a great deal.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>

  <h2>A disease of the cell's antenna</h2>
  <p>
    BBS is what doctors call a <strong>ciliopathy</strong>. Nearly every cell in the body
    carries a single <strong>primary cilium</strong> — a slender, antenna-like projection it
    uses to sense its surroundings and receive signals. BBS is caused by mutations in a set
    of genes (more than twenty are now known) that build a protein machine called the
    <strong>BBSome</strong>, whose job is to traffic specific receptors out to that antenna
    and manage what sits on it.<Cite n={2} /> Because cilia are everywhere, the damage is
    everywhere: the light-sensing cells of the retina degenerate, causing progressive sight
    loss; the kidneys, limbs, and reproductive system develop abnormally; and the appetite
    system misfires. The classic features include rod–cone retinal dystrophy, obesity,
    postaxial polydactyly (extra fingers or toes), kidney anomalies, hypogonadism, and
    learning difficulties.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>

  <h2>Why the antenna matters for appetite</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the connection to this site. The appetite neurons of the hypothalamus do not read{" "}
    <a href="/system">leptin</a> and melanocortin signals from just anywhere on their surface
    — the relevant receptors, including the machinery of the <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a>{' '}
    appetite pathway, need to be positioned on the primary cilium to work properly. The BBSome
    is what ferries them there. When the BBSome is faulty, those receptors never reach the
    antenna; they are left stranded in the cell body, and the leptin–melanocortin signal that
    should restrain hunger is simply not received.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>
  <p>
    So the break in BBS is subtly different from the one in POMC deficiency, and the difference
    is worth holding onto. In POMC deficiency the <em>signal</em> is missing. In BBS the signal
    is made and released, but there is no properly placed <em>receiver</em>. Both roads arrive
    at the same destination: an MC4R pathway that is not being driven, and an appetite with no
    brake.
  </p>

