    source="Ciliopathy mechanism and clinical features per Forsythe & Beales, Eur J Hum Genet 2013 (PMID 22713813). Schematic, not to cellular scale."
  >
    <CiliaAntenna />
  </Figure>

  <h2>The treatment, and what it reaches</h2>
  <p>
    Because the problem ends at an under-driven MC4R, a drug that drives MC4R directly can help
    — regardless of why the receptor is quiet. <a href="/setmelanotide">Setmelanotide</a>, the
    MC4R agonist, re-supplies the appetite signal downstream of the ciliary defect. In a Phase 3
    randomised, placebo-controlled trial in people with BBS, it produced meaningful weight loss
    and reductions in hunger, and on that basis the FDA approved it for Bardet–Biedl syndrome in
    <strong>2022</strong> — its second major indication after the{" "}
    <a href="/pomc-deficiency">POMC and leptin-receptor deficiencies</a>.<Cite n={1} /><Cite n={3} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The limit is as important as the result. Setmelanotide treats the obesity and the hunger of
    BBS; it does nothing for the retinal degeneration, the kidney disease, or the other
    consequences of broken cilia, which are managed separately. It is a targeted fix for the one
    arm of a many-armed syndrome that happens to run through the melanocortin system.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Bardet–Biedl syndrome earns its place here because it widens the definition of a
    "melanocortin disease." You do not have to delete the signal or its receptor to break the
    appetite circuit — you only have to stop the receptor from reaching the antenna where it
    listens. That the same drug helps across POMC deficiency, leptin-receptor deficiency, and a
    ciliopathy is the strongest evidence that MC4R is the true common chokepoint of human
    appetite. For the drug, see <a href="/setmelanotide">setmelanotide</a>; for the receptor,
    see <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a>; for the conditions it shares a mechanism with, see{" "}
    <a href="/pomc-deficiency">POMC &amp; leptin-receptor deficiency</a> and{" "}
    <a href="/hypothalamic-obesity">hypothalamic obesity</a>.
  </p>

